"""Additional original Rust incident exercises, all explicitly fictional."""
def extend(items):
    def add(slug,title,lesson,plain,context,code,cause,fix,lab,verify,source):
        items.append(dict(id=slug,title=title,lesson=lesson,category='Rust in practice',kind='Practice scenario',reviewed='2026-10-31',
          simple=plain,context=context,syntax=code,evidence='Illustrative code sketch — not a standalone executable\n'+code,syntax_label='Illustrative Rust sketch · surrounding application code omitted',
          symptom=context,diagnosis='Trace the ownership and control flow in the example. '+cause,cause=cause,fix=fix,verify=verify,
          prevention='Keep a regression for this failure and document the resource or data contract. '+verify,
          question='Explain why the proposed change fixes this failure and name one limit of the fix.',source=source,
          task=lab,simple_lab=lab,investigation_questions=['What did the caller expect?', 'Which step breaks that expectation?', 'What evidence would prove the fix works?'],
          timeline=['Expected behavior: '+plain,'Observed behavior: '+context,'Investigation finding: '+cause],
          simulation='Fictional incident exercise. The application and observations are invented for practice; the linked Rust documentation describes the relevant behavior.',
          ticket=f'RB-{len(items)+1:03d}'))
    add('unpolled-task','A reminder is never sent','futures',
      'Preparing a task is not the same as carrying it out. A future needs to be driven before its work happens.',
      'A calendar endpoint creates a reminder future and immediately reports success. No mail request appears in the test transport. The future is dropped when the handler returns.',
      'let reminder = send_reminder(user);\ndrop(reminder); // nothing drove the future',
      'The async body was never polled. Constructing the future did not send anything.',
      'Await the operation when the response promises completion, or explicitly schedule and observe a task. For a durable background promise, persist the job before acknowledging it.',
      'Replace the mail sender with an async test function that records one call. Compare constructing it with awaiting it.',
      'Assert the sender runs exactly once on success and that its error reaches the caller. A spawned in-memory task still does not survive process exit.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/future/trait.Future.html')
    add('ignored-join','A background worker fails silently','tokio',
      'A helper can fail even when the main request looks successful. Someone must collect and inspect the helper’s result.',
      'A report service spawns a task, discards its handle, and returns a download link. Some tasks panic or return storage errors; the link is created although no report exists.',
      'tokio::spawn(async move { write_report().await });\n// The result is never observed.',
      'The caller treats starting work as successful completion and ignores the task outcome.',
      'Keep and await the JoinHandle when completion is required, handling both JoinError and the operation Result. Otherwise persist explicit queued, running, failed, and completed states.',
      'Make a worker return an error and write a caller that checks both the join outcome and the inner Result.',
      'Test both a worker panic and an ordinary error; neither should produce a completed report state.',
      'https://tokio.rs/tokio/tutorial/spawning')
    add('sender-held','A worker never notices shutdown','channels',
      'A receiver knows sending is finished only after every sender is gone and queued messages are drained.',
      'A CLI joins its worker during exit, but the worker stays inside recv. A forgotten sender clone is still stored in the configuration object.',
      'let extra_sender = sender.clone();\ndrop(sender);\n// recv can still wait because extra_sender exists.',
      'Dropping one sender does not disconnect a channel while another sender remains alive.',
      'Make sender ownership explicit and drop every producer endpoint before waiting for the draining receiver, or use an explicit shutdown message with a defined protocol.',
      'Use recv_timeout to compare the state with one sender still alive and the state after all senders are dropped.',
      'Distinguish timeout from disconnection, and verify queued messages are processed before termination.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/mpsc/')
    add('refcell-reentry','A screen crashes while updating its model','refcell',
      'RefCell lets you borrow at runtime, but it still allows only one writer or shared readers at a time.',
      'A desktop callback keeps a read guard alive while calling another callback that asks to change the same cell. The second callback panics with a borrow error.',
      'let view = model.borrow();\nnotify_observers(); // callback calls model.borrow_mut()\nuse_view(&view);',
      'The read borrow overlaps a reentrant mutable borrow. Runtime checking detects the conflict.',
      'Copy the small notification data, end the guard’s scope, then invoke callbacks. Use try_borrow_mut only when the application has a meaningful deferral or rejection policy.',
      'Hold a shared borrow, show that try_borrow_mut fails, then drop the guard and show that it succeeds.',
      'Test a callback that reenters the model and assert the state update is neither lost nor performed twice.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/cell/struct.RefCell.html')
    add('clone-cost','Copying request data makes a service slow','ownership',
      'A clone can copy a large amount of data. Copying just to make the compiler quiet may hide an expensive design.',
      'An upload handler clones a large Vec for each validation step. It works correctly but memory and latency grow as more validators are enabled.',
      'validate_header(payload.clone());\nvalidate_body(payload.clone());',
      'Each validator accepts ownership even though it only reads the bytes, causing avoidable full-buffer copies.',
      'Let read-only validators accept &[u8]. Move ownership once into the final consumer. Use shared owned storage only when independent longer-lived tasks need it.',
      'Write two validators taking &[u8] and call both with one vector without cloning it.',
      'Retain correctness tests and measure allocations with realistic payload sizes before and after the change.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch04-02-references-and-borrowing.html')
    add('lock-order','Two transfers wait forever','arc-mutex',
      'Two workers can each hold something the other needs. Neither can finish unless the locking plan changes.',
      'One transfer locks account A then B. A simultaneous reverse transfer locks B then A. Each worker waits for the other account while keeping its first guard.',
      '// Worker 1: lock(A), then lock(B)\n// Worker 2: lock(B), then lock(A)',
      'Opposite lock acquisition orders allow a cycle of waiting.',
      'Order account locks by a stable key on every path, or protect the operation with one suitable aggregate lock. Handle the same-account case separately.',
      'Write the lock order for transfers 1→2 and 2→1 using increasing account IDs; show that both orders agree.',
      'Stress opposing transfers with a bounded test deadline and check both completion and balance conservation.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Mutex.html')
    add('unwrapped-input','One bad form value crashes a request','result',
      'A visitor typing the wrong kind of value is normal. The program should explain the problem rather than panic.',
      'An age field reaches parse().unwrap(). A user submits text instead of digits and the request panics. Successful-only tests never exercised that path.',
      'let age: u8 = input.parse().unwrap();',
      'unwrap assumes success even though the string comes from an untrusted boundary.',
      'Match the parse Result and return a clear validation error. Check domain limits after parsing, and keep internal details separate from the external response.',
      'Write a parser accepting "12" and returning Err for "hello" or a value that does not fit u8.',
      'Check valid input, malformed input, and out-of-range input without catching panics as the ordinary validation strategy.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch09-02-recoverable-errors-with-result.html')
    add('money-float','A bill has inconsistent rounding','floats',
      'Decimal-looking floating-point values can be approximations. Exact money rules need an explicit representation and rounding policy.',
      'A receipt service adds decimal amounts with f64 and compares totals exactly. Different calculation paths disagree by a tiny amount and trigger a mismatch.',
      'let total = 0.1_f64 + 0.2_f64;\nlet equal = total == 0.3_f64;',
      'The representation does not store every decimal fraction exactly, while the business rule expects exact decimal accounting.',
      'Use checked integer minor units when appropriate, or a deliberate decimal library and documented rounding rule. Validate currency scale and overflow.',
      'Represent 10 cents plus 20 cents as integers and check the exact total of 30.',
      'Test rounding boundaries, refunds, different currency scales, and overflow. A wider float does not establish a money policy.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/primitive.f64.html')
    add('hash-key','A lookup cannot find an inserted key','maps',
      'A map depends on stable rules for recognizing its keys. Changing those rules while a key is stored breaks that assumption.',
      'A cache key uses interior mutable data in its hash calculation. Updating that data after insertion makes later lookups unreliable.',
      '// insert key whose hash depends on mutable state\n// mutate that state while the key is still in the map\n// lookup uses a different hash',
      'The key’s Hash or Eq behavior changes while stored, violating the map’s expected key contract.',
      'Use immutable key identity. Remove and reinsert under a new key when identity changes, and ensure equal keys hash consistently.',
      'Define an immutable key from tenant ID and item ID, insert it, and retrieve it using an equal key.',
      'Test equal-key lookup and distinguish identity changes from changes to the associated value.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/struct.HashMap.html')
    add('ignored-iterator','A cleanup step does nothing','iterators',
      'A list of planned operations does no work until something asks for its results.',
      'A batch cleanup builds a map iterator that calls a removal function, then discards it. The job reports completion but the records were never visited.',
      'let work = ids.iter().map(|id| remove_record(id));\ndrop(work);',
      'map is lazy; no consumer requested the next item.',
      'Use an explicit loop with error handling, or consume the iterator through an appropriate Result-aware operation. Do not ignore per-item errors.',
      'Replace removal with a counter in a test. Compare an unused map with a for loop and check the number of visits.',
      'Assert every intended item is processed and an injected failure produces the documented partial-work behavior.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html')
    add('index-panic','An empty result crashes the response','vectors',
      'A list may contain no items. Asking for its first item must handle that possibility.',
      'An endpoint indexes results[0] after a database search. It works for populated test data but panics when a customer has no matching records.',
      'let newest = results[0];',
      'The code assumes the collection is nonempty without establishing that invariant.',
      'Use first or get and choose an explicit empty-state response. If nonempty data is a domain invariant, validate it at construction.',
      'Use first on an empty vector and a one-element vector, then handle both outcomes.',
      'Test empty, single, and multiple result sets and ensure the response contract distinguishes absence from server failure.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/vec/struct.Vec.html')
    add('endianness','A device reports impossible packet sizes','network-framing',
      'Computers must agree on the order of bytes in a number. A protocol should specify that order rather than use each machine’s native choice.',
      'A sensor gateway reads a two-byte network length using native byte order. A packet from another platform appears much larger than its actual payload.',
      'let length = u16::from_ne_bytes([header[0], header[1]]);',
      'The decoder uses host byte order instead of the wire format’s specified order.',
      'Use from_be_bytes or from_le_bytes according to the protocol, then enforce a maximum length before allocating.',
      'Decode [0, 5] as big-endian and verify the length is five regardless of host architecture.',
      'Retain known wire fixtures and test maximum lengths, incomplete headers, and truncated payloads.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/primitive.u16.html')
    add('partial-write','A saved record is cut short','io',
      'A write call may accept fewer bytes than you offered. A successful call is not always a complete write.',
      'A log exporter calls write once and assumes the whole record was stored. Under a test writer that accepts small chunks, the output is missing its tail.',
      'let written = writer.write(record)?;\n// written is ignored',
      'The returned count is ignored even though Write::write may perform a partial write.',
      'Use write_all when all bytes must be submitted, or loop over the remaining slice. Separately define flushing and durability requirements.',
      'Use a test writer that accepts at most two bytes per call and verify write_all sends a longer record completely.',
      'Test partial writes and injected errors. Successful write_all alone does not prove bytes survived a crash on durable storage.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/io/trait.Write.html')
    add('text-buffer-growth','The second input includes the first','io',
      'Reading into a String can add new text to what is already there. Reusing the buffer needs an explicit reset when each input is separate.',
      'A command-line tool accepts the first number, but the second becomes a combination of both entered lines. The same String is reused for read_line without clearing it.',
      'loop {\n    stdin.read_line(&mut input)?;\n    process(&input);\n}',
      'read_line appends to the existing buffer instead of replacing its contents.',
      'Clear the input buffer before reading the next independent line and handle a zero-byte read as EOF. Keep parsing separate from input acquisition.',
      'Append two lines to a String, then repeat while clearing it between reads and compare the parsed values.',
      'Test two consecutive inputs, an empty line, invalid text, and EOF without an infinite loop.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/io/trait.BufRead.html')
    add('pooled-connection','The pool runs out of available connections','database',
      'A limited resource must be returned when work no longer needs it. Holding it while waiting on something else can block everyone behind you.',
      'A Rust API borrows a database connection, reads one row, then holds the connection across a slow HTTP call. New requests time out waiting for pool capacity.',
      'let connection = pool.acquire().await?;\nlet row = read_row(&connection).await?;\nlet reply = remote_call(row).await?;\ndrop(connection);',
      'The database resource remains owned during unrelated remote waiting.',
      'Convert the needed result into owned data and end the connection’s scope before remote I/O when transaction semantics allow. Use a durable workflow if several systems must coordinate.',
      'Draw the resource lifetime and move the release point before the simulated slow call without losing required data.',
      'Measure connection wait time and transaction duration under slow downstream behavior. Do not shorten scope by breaking a required atomic operation.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch04-01-what-is-ownership.html')
    add('ffi-panic','A foreign call terminates the process','ffi',
      'A caller written in another language does not automatically understand Rust’s panic behavior.',
      'A C-facing Rust function unwraps a failed parse. The host application unexpectedly terminates when it supplies malformed text.',
      'extern "C" fn parse_value(/* foreign arguments */) {\n    // fallible operation followed by unwrap\n}',
      'The interface exposes a panic path across an ABI that does not permit ordinary Rust unwinding.',
      'Validate foreign inputs, return a documented status or result representation, and prevent unwinding from crossing an incompatible boundary. Do not treat catch_unwind as protection against all faults or aborting panics.',
      'Write a safe internal parser returning Result and sketch how a C wrapper maps each variant to a status code.',
      'Test malformed input through the foreign interface and confirm a controlled error rather than termination.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/nomicon/ffi.html')
    add('stale-result','An older search replaces a newer result','async-locks',
      'Two requests may finish in a different order from the order they started.',
      'A search UI starts a slow request for the first typed word and a faster request for the updated word. The slow response arrives last and overwrites the current results.',
      '// request 1 starts\n// request 2 starts and finishes\n// request 1 finishes and replaces visible state',
      'Completion order is mistaken for request order; there is no freshness check before updating shared state.',
      'Associate each request with a generation and only apply results for the current generation. Cancellation can reduce wasted work but freshness checks still protect races.',
      'Simulate responses arriving in reverse order and apply only the response whose generation matches the current search.',
      'Test reverse completion, cancellation, and an older failure arriving after a newer success.',
      'https://tokio.rs/tokio/tutorial/select')
    add('cache-unbounded','A cache grows until the process runs out of memory','maps',
      'Remembering every item forever is not a bounded cache. A cache needs a rule for when to forget.',
      'A service keeps each distinct requested key in a HashMap. Traffic with many unique keys grows memory even though repeated-key tests stay small.',
      'cache.insert(user_supplied_key, response);\n// no capacity or expiry policy',
      'The key space is unbounded and stored entries are never evicted.',
      'Define capacity and expiry behavior, bound key and value sizes, and choose an eviction strategy. Include in-flight fills in the memory budget.',
      'Build a tiny bounded cache model and show what happens when a fourth key arrives at a capacity of three.',
      'Test many distinct keys and concurrent misses. Memory must plateau according to a measured bound, not merely a count of entries.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/collections/struct.HashMap.html')
    add('wall-clock','A measured duration suddenly becomes invalid','profiling',
      'A wall clock tells the time of day and can be adjusted. Measuring elapsed work needs a clock designed for elapsed time.',
      'A latency recorder subtracts SystemTime values. After a clock adjustment, duration_since reports an error and an unwrap causes a panic.',
      'let start = std::time::SystemTime::now();\n// later: now.duration_since(start).unwrap()',
      'Wall-clock time is assumed to move steadily forward, and its fallible difference is unwrapped.',
      'Use Instant for elapsed durations within one process and handle SystemTime differences when civil timestamps are required.',
      'Time a small local operation with Instant, then describe why a stored calendar timestamp still needs SystemTime or a date-time representation.',
      'Inject a controllable clock into timestamp-dependent rules and test backward adjustments without relying on changing the machine clock.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/time/struct.Instant.html')
    add('send-after-close','A worker panics when the receiver disappears','channels',
      'Sending a message can fail if no receiver remains. That can be a normal part of shutdown.',
      'A worker calls send(...).unwrap() after a client disconnects and drops the receiver. The resulting panic makes a routine cancellation look like a service defect.',
      'sender.send(result).unwrap();',
      'Channel disconnection is treated as an impossible condition even though cancellation can legitimately cause it.',
      'Handle SendError according to ownership of the work: discard an unneeded response, report a failed delivery, or persist a job outcome. Do not blindly ignore a message that represents required durable work.',
      'Create a channel, drop the receiver, and match the error returned by send without panicking.',
      'Test disconnection before and after work completes and verify the chosen loss or retry policy.',
      'https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/mpsc/')

    intros={
      'graphql-body':'A server can run out of room if it accepts a message with no size limit. Like an overflowing bag, more input needs more memory.',
      'build-secret':'A build is also a program. If it prints private settings, saved build files may contain those settings even when the finished app never logs them.',
      'ffi-server-name':'One language may keep a pointer after the other language has thrown the data away. Both sides must agree how long that data stays available.',
      'markdown-expansion':'A small input can create a huge amount of work. Limiting input size alone does not always limit the expanded result.',
      'tls-loop':'A connection can get stuck inside its security setup. The important question is whether it is waiting normally or repeating work without a stopping path.',
      'http2-flood':'A few connections can make a server spend too much time on protocol work, leaving ordinary visitors waiting.',
      'http2-queue':'A server may keep preparing responses that a peer refuses to receive. The hidden queue needs a limit too.',
      'lock-await':'A worker keeps the only key to shared data while waiting for another service. Other workers cannot use the data until that key is returned.',
      'executor-starved':'A long calculation occupies a worker that was meant to help many waiting tasks. Marking the function async does not move the calculation elsewhere.',
      'queue-oom':'Jobs arrive faster than workers finish them. An unlimited waiting line can use all available memory.',
      'cancelled-write':'A timeout means you stopped waiting. It does not prove the other service stopped working, so a retry can repeat an action.',
      'shutdown-loss':'Closing the front door is not the same as finishing the work inside. Background jobs need a plan when the program stops.',
      'unicode-panic':'A visible letter or emoji can use several bytes. Cutting text at an arbitrary byte can cut through its encoding.',
      'overflow-release':'A number type has a maximum size. Adding beyond that limit needs a deliberate rule instead of an accidental result.',
      'rc-leak':'Two objects can keep each other alive even after the rest of the program stops using them. A weak back-link can break that ownership circle.',
      'sqlite-busy':'SQLite allows one writer at a time. Keeping a write transaction open during slow work makes other writers wait.',
      'lost-update':'Two workers read the same old value and both write a new one. One update can hide the other unless the database enforces the rule.',
      'flaky-order':'A HashMap does not promise a sorted order. If customers need a sorted report, the program must create that order deliberately.',
      'poisoned-lock':'A panic happened while shared data was being changed. The lock warns that the data might now be only partly updated.',
      'duplicate-dependency':'A project can contain two versions of the same dependency. Updating one path does not automatically repair the other.'}
    for item in items:
        item.setdefault('simple',intros.get(item['id'],item['context']))
        item.setdefault('syntax',item['evidence'])
        item.setdefault('syntax_label','Illustrative evidence · not executable code')
        item.setdefault('simple_lab',item['task'])
